module gunbc.provisioning.ubuntu_autoinstall_seed

// THE SEED AN ALTRA BOARD NET-BOOTS INTO (physical-machine boot program, cut 6, step 2: "OS
// installation through autoinstall from a pinned origin"). extdeps.os.ubuntu_autoinstall owns what
// SUBIQUITY accepts; this module owns what WE choose to send it, for a board with no panel, no
// keyboard, and no operator who could answer a question. What separates this seed from the NOCLOUD one
// extdeps.provisioning.ubuntu_seeded_install_media builds is not its content but its audience: that
// seed is carried inside an image and its author had a console nearby, this one is fetched at boot by a
// machine nobody is watching. A question in a seed is therefore not a delay, it is a hung install until
// a timeout fires — which makes "PROVEN ANSWERLESS" the property, not "written carefully".
//
// WHO CONSUMES THIS, declared per DESIGN 3c rather than left to be inferred.
//   * CONSUMED IN THIS CHANGE: every refusal arm, both digest functions, the render, the audit, the selection
//     rule and the projection edge are consumed by test.claim.ubuntu_autoinstall_seed_witness. Nothing here is
//     exercised only by its own module. The roster and its verdicts are OBTAINED, NEVER QUOTED here (DESIGN
//     section 6: name the instrument, never transcribe its output): the count of test fns is derived from the
//     witness file by gunbc.frontend_eval.control's test_fns, which reads the module-scope `test fn ` lines the
//     parser itself requires, and each one's verdict comes from the claim run, which reports one PASS or FAIL
//     line per test fn — `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry
//     dag/test/claim/ubuntu_autoinstall_seed_witness.dag --claim-run --function <the fn>`. A number written into
//     this comment would be a second, rotting copy of a fact both of those produce on demand; if the two ever
//     disagree, the module has grown a rule nothing exercises and the run says so, not this sentence.
//   * CONSUMED ON MAIN: test.claim.ubuntu_autoinstall_seed_witness, as above. That is the only EXECUTING consumer
//     of anything here while this change stands on its own, and this header says so rather than borrowing a
//     sibling's unmerged branch for standing.
//   * DECLARED FRONTIER, NAMED, WITH ITS TRIGGER — NOT CITED AS JUSTIFICATION: the binding record is imported
//     (not yet executed) by the S5 sibling, gunbc.provisioning.provisioning_arm, whose branch imports
//     UbuntuAutoinstallSeedBinding. Naming that import as this module's consumer would be circular — S5 resolves
//     only because S4 exists, so S5's branch cannot be what justifies S4 (review: swift-wren-451; the PR's
//     earlier wording did exactly that and is withdrawn here). What is true and checkable on main is the
//     frontier itself: cut 6 step 3 must mint the install-attempt record through bind_ubuntu_autoinstall_seed and
//     read binding_digest — if step 3 lands and never calls this module, these declarations are scaffold and must
//     be deleted, which is the same rule that already governs project_autoinstall_identity and
//     project_storage_policy: consumed by cut 6 step 4 (a seed becomes bytes an installer can fetch via
//     extdeps.os.ubuntu_autoinstall.UbuntuAutoinstallPayload), and until that step lands they have a witness but
//     no production caller — which is why they are the frontier and not the product.
//   * NOT A FRONTIER: nothing else. A module that later wants a seed must import this one rather than copy it —
//     the review already caught one duplicated spelling of these names, and a fork is that defect with more
//     files.
//
// WHY gunbc.* AND NOT extdeps.*. Nothing below is a fact about UBUNTU. The package set, the disk-selection
// rule, the credential posture and the binding are decisions this repository makes about its own fleet;
// Canonical authors none of them, so they are not evidence about an external thing and do not belong in the
// extdeps layer. The external shapes (extdeps.os.ubuntu_autoinstall, extdeps.storage.types,
// extdeps.ssh.keys, extdeps.bmc.endpoint) are IMPORTED and projected into, never restated — if a future edit
// re-declares what upstream already defines, that is the boundary being crossed, and the fix is an import,
// not a copy.
//
// ONE THING BELOW *IS* AN EXTERNAL FACT, AND SAYS SO (review 81975). The sha512-crypt storage format — the
// scheme token, the salt's width and forbidden characters, the digest's alphabet and its exact 86-character
// width, and the cost field's class and range — belongs to libxcrypt, not to this repository or to Canonical.
// It is therefore NOT stated here: it is one row in extdeps.libxcrypt.crypt_scheme, cited to the crypt(5)
// mdoc source at the release this distro ships (libcrypt1 1:4.4.36-4build1 on Noble arm64), and consumed
// below by name. What stays
// in this module is only what is ours: the segment grammar we walk, the order the refusals are reported in,
// and which credential we will accept for a headless board. If a future edit re-types 86 or 16 here instead
// of reading them from that row, the figures and their citation have come apart, which is the §4d failure the
// standing field exists to make visible.
//
// A SECOND EXTERNAL FACT SITS ON ITS OWN SHELF FOR THE SAME REASON, AND IT ARRIVED LATE (review 83132). The
// debconf selection line — four fields in a fixed order, separated by runs of whitespace, with nine legal type
// words and a flag that marks rather than answers — is a fact about debconf, versioned with debconf, and
// Canonical does not author it either. It is extdeps.debian.debconf, cited to the release Noble ships
// (debconf 1.5.86ubuntu1), consumed below by name. What stays here is ours: WHICH questions must be answered,
// what counts as an answer for this programme (stricter than upstream on the empty value, and the difference is
// stated at the shelf's constructor), and the silence that must be proven rather than assumed. The earlier
// revision of this module kept that grammar in a tokenizer here, which is why the comment-line defect could
// exist at all: any fix was a fix to a copy. If a future edit re-spells "select" or re-splits a line here, it
// has re-minted an upstream fact and the shelf's citation is now a comment.
//
// NO MACHINE IS NAMED AND NO DELIVERY IS CHOSEN HERE, and both refusals are structural rather than
// stylistic, each witnessed. What is absent, precisely: NO FIELD CARRIES THE TARGET UNIT'S OWN LABEL, and
// no decision in this module reads a string. A hostname in a seed is a per-unit special case wearing a
// config field, which is the class of undeclared rule this whole program exists to retire; two units of
// the same model must reach the same digest. Two host-bearing fields exist and are NOT that thing:
// `ArchiveMirrorAuthority`'s `host` names the ARCHIVE we fetch from (shared by every unit, and its
// absence is a refusal, not a default), and `project_autoinstall_identity`'s `binding_hostname` is a
// PARAMETER the binding layer supplies into a field upstream's payload demands — this module never
// invents it, never branches on it, and does not store it in the seed, so it cannot reach the digest.
// Delivery arrives as DATA (the S0 seam's decision, or a caller's strategy record) and its only
// consequence here is the console stanza, which is DERIVED from the strategy rather than stored beside it
// — so a strategy name cannot quietly change what the seed promises.

